### User: How do I take each of these and combine them into a single regEx expression?

```
var t = "<test>";
t = t.replace(/^</, '');
t = t.replace(/>+$/, '');

```

which results in t equal to test without the <>

 -

### Top Answer: You can use pipe `|`. In regex it means OR:

```
t = "<test>>".replace(/^<|>+$/g, "");
// "test"

```

But of course, you can use another ways like:

```
t = "<test>>".replace(/[<>]/g, "");
// "test"

```

or even with match:

```
t = "<test>>".match(/\w+/)[0];
// "test"

```

Make sure you've added the `g`-global flag when needed. This flag stands for *all occurrences*.

